{
  "$comment": "All 92 Indiana counties, each levying a mandatory local income tax (LIT) -- unlike Michigan (24-city closed list) or Ohio (municipality-by-municipality), EVERY Indiana address falls inside exactly one of these 92 rates. There is no zero-rate default case. Rates transcribed directly from the Indiana Department of Revenue's own semi-annual Departmental Notice #1, not a secondary aggregator.",
  "year": 2025,
  "asOf": "2026-09-29",
  "effectiveFrom": "2025-01-01",
  "source": {
    "title": "Departmental Notice #1 -- How to Compute Withholding for State and County Income Tax, effective Jan. 1, 2025 (R45 / 12-24), Internet Archive copy of in.gov/dor/files/dn01.pdf captured 2025-03-13",
    "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250313123314id_/https://www.in.gov/dor/files/dn01.pdf",
    "verifiedOn": "2026-09-29",
    "verifiedBy": "All 92 rows parsed from the archived PDF text; county codes 01-92 contiguous and names match the 2026 file. The October 2025 notice (R46 / 10-25, archived 2025-11-08: https://web.archive.org/web/20251108221909id_/https://www.in.gov/dor/files/dn01.pdf) lists identical rates for every county with no asterisks, so one table covers all of 2025. The same notice gives the 2025 state rate: 'For 2025, the state adjusted gross income tax rate for individuals is 3.00%.'"
  },
  "rateBasis": {
    "rule": "Indiana LIT is determined by COUNTY OF RESIDENCE as of January 1 of the tax year -- not county of work, which is the opposite default from Ohio's workplace-sourced municipal tax. If an individual resides in an Indiana county on Jan 1, that county's rate applies regardless of where they work (even in a different Indiana county, or out of state). ONLY if the individual resides OUT OF STATE on Jan 1 does the fallback apply: the rate of their Indiana county of principal work/business as of Jan 1.",
    "quotedFromSource": "Both the county of residence and the county of principal business or employment of an individual are determined on Jan. 1 of the calendar year... If an individual resides in an Indiana county on Jan. 1, the rate corresponds to that county of residence. If the [individual] resides out-of-state on Jan. 1 but has his or her principal place of work or business in an Indiana county as of Jan. 1, he or she is subject to county tax at the rate corresponding to that Indiana county.",
    "mechanism": "Captured on Form WH-4 (Employee's Withholding Exemption AND COUNTY STATUS Certificate) -- the county-status determination is built into the same certificate as the exemption count, not a separate form.",
    "updateCadence": "Rates are republished TWICE A YEAR (Jan. 1 and Oct. 1) via a new Departmental Notice #1. This snapshot is the Jan. 1, 2026 table (R46/01-26); the immediately prior table was Oct. 1, 2025 (R45) -- see priorTable below, now captured."
  },
  "reciprocityInteraction": {
    "rule": "Reciprocity (see reciprocity in IN-2026.json) exempts a qualifying reciprocal-state resident from Indiana STATE tax only. Indiana COUNTY tax still applies and must still be withheld. Confirmed directly from Form WH-47 itself: \"Note: The employee understands that the employer remains responsible for withholding any applicable Indiana County taxes.\" This is a real, easy-to-miss trap: implementing reciprocity as a blanket Indiana-withholding exemption would be wrong for the county portion."
  },
